Law: 21 CFR 1306.12(a). Schedule II prescriptions cannot have refills.
Every matching medication line needs its own confirmed zero; another line's
zero, an unconfirmed default, and a confirmed nonzero value do not count.
Source: https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-21/section-1306.12 [lean/Ehr/Specs/V1.lean:scheduleIIRefillsEntry]

Hospital policy (not law): local signing workflow; the Schedule II and naloxone rules retain their separate legal bases.
What signing medication orders needs: patient-ID re-entry, today's weight in kg, the dosing
weight, and every order-line element on every line. When an opioid order
carries a higher overdose risk, a naloxone offer and overdose education are
also needed (CA Business and Professions Code §741).

Order content: 22 CCR 70263(g), (h)(4); 42 CFR 482.24(c)(2), 482.23(c)(3). Re-entry and kg-only checks are our safety policy, not statutory fields. Our naloxone gate is stricter than B&P 741: facility-administered orders are exempt in law; our model also treats unknown dose/frequency as risk (docs/regulations.md §3.1, C2). [lean/Ehr/Specs/V1.lean:orderSigningEntries]

Hospital policy (not law): local pre-op signing gate supporting consent and H&P duties.
Pre-op orders also need consent verified by a human and an updated H&P,
and a decision about which home medications to hold.

Consent and H&P: 42 CFR 482.51(b)(1)–(2); 22 CCR 70223(d). The pre-op signing deadline and meds-to-hold field are our stricter policy (docs/regulations.md §3.2 R6). [lean/Ehr/Specs/V1.lean:preOpExtraEntries]

Hospital policy (not law): local note section and billing checklist supporting record authentication duties.
A signed visit or consult note needs the core note sections, the diagnosis
(once for the visit and once for the claim), the level of service, follow-up,
instructions, and at least the first medication-reconciliation decision.

Record authentication: 42 CFR 482.24(b), (c)(1); 22 CCR 70751(g). This precise section list, duplicate claim diagnosis and billing prompts are our design (docs/regulations.md E20, H1). [lean/Ehr/Specs/V1.lean:signNoteEntries]

Hospital policy (not law): local result acknowledgment and patient-message workflow.
Acknowledging a result needs a decision. For an abnormal result, the
patient must also be sent a message.

CA H&S 123148(e) requires timely reporting; our abnormal-result message and acknowledgment gate are design choices. Special electronic-release restrictions in 123148(f) are not modeled (docs/regulations.md E19, R4). [lean/Ehr/Specs/V1.lean:ackResultEntries]

Hospital policy (not law): local refill protocol for visit/lab monitoring and prescription completeness.
A refill decision needs the decision, the quantity, the number of refills,
the pharmacy, and the protocol check.

Our clinical protocol, not a legal lab/visit requirement (docs/regulations.md §3.1 E17). [lean/Ehr/Specs/V1.lean:approveRefillEntries]

Law: 42 USC 300aa-25(a), 300aa-26(d); site/route gating is hospital policy.
Recording a vaccine needs the vaccine, lot, site, route, and the VIS.

42 USC 300aa-25(a) covers record content and 300aa-26(d) VIS before administration. Site/route gating is our policy. The VIS edition date, manufacturer and provider are required; this demo still does not model every statutory field or establish actual VIS delivery timing (docs/regulations.md E11). [lean/Ehr/Specs/V1.lean:recordImmunizationEntries]

Hospital policy (not law): local ED disposition documentation checklist.
The ED disposition needs the choice, the diagnosis, and the condition.

Our ED workflow gate, modeled on discharge record requirements; no law mandates this exact screen or field list (docs/regulations.md E20). [lean/Ehr/Specs/V1.lean:dispositionEntries]

Every entry requirement in spec v1. [lean/Ehr/Specs/V1.lean:entries]

Law: CA H&S 11165.4(a), (c); the modeled review gate is stricter.
Before signing a Schedule II–IV controlled substance: the CURES report,
shown or reviewed by a prescriber in the last 24 hours, unless the prescriber
records a statutory exemption (CA Health and Safety Code §11165.4).

CA H&S 11165.4(a)(1)–(2), (c). Law requires first prescribing and six-month rechecks, with data obtained within 24 h or the previous business day. Our every-signing, 24 h-only, prescriber-only display/receipt gate is stricter and does not model delegation or all exemptions (docs/regulations.md §2.2, C1). [lean/Ehr/Specs/V1.lean:curesReview]

Law: 21 CFR 1311.140(a)(1), (3)–(5), referencing 1311.120(b)(9).
The patient and prescription details (drug, dose, quantity, directions, refills)
stay shown while signing a controlled prescription. No receipt or waiver
substitutes for display. The interpreter also refuses macro commits
(`Core/Interp.lean`, `clickCore`: `human only: commit`; T3/T6).
Applying this to facility orders too is our stricter demo policy. This is not
a model of DEA two-factor authentication or full EPCS compliance.
Source: https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-21/chapter-II/part-1311/subpart-C/section-1311.140 [lean/Ehr/Specs/V1.lean:controlledRxReview]

Hospital policy (not law): local allergy, medication and dose review protocol; CURES and EPCS retain separate legal bases.
Before signing medication orders: the allergy list and current medications
(shown, or reviewed by a prescriber in the last 4 hours), the dose check
(shown for weight-based orders), and, for controlled substances, the CURES report.

45 CFR 170.315(a)(4) requires automatic drug checks in certified technology, not these list displays. List display, roles and receipt windows are our design. Dose display follows ISMP kg guidance; no-receipt is our choice (docs/regulations.md R2–3). Controlled-prescription details remain displayed during signing under 21 CFR 1311.140(a)(3)–(4); applying this no-receipt gate to every controlled order, including facility orders, is our stricter demo policy. [lean/Ehr/Specs/V1.lean:orderSigningReviews]

Hospital policy (not law): local pre-op anticoagulant review and sign-screen placement.
Before signing pre-op orders: also the anticoagulant list, always shown.

TJC NPG anticoagulant protocols support review; sign-screen display is our policy, not law (docs/regulations.md R5). [lean/Ehr/Specs/V1.lean:preOpExtraReviews]

Hospital policy (not law): local abnormal-result trend display and acknowledgment workflow.
Before acknowledging a result: the result itself, and its trend when it is
abnormal. Both shown on the screen; no receipt counts.

SAFER Test Results supports acknowledgment; trend display and no-receipt are our choices. CLIA 42 CFR 493.1291(g) binds the lab, not this screen (docs/regulations.md R4). [lean/Ehr/Specs/V1.lean:ackResultReviews]

Hospital policy (not law): local refill monitoring protocol; receipt windows are product design.
Before a refill decision: the relevant labs (always shown), and the last
visit and fill history (shown, or reviewed by a prescriber in the last 7 days).

Our clinical protocol and receipt windows, not law (docs/regulations.md E17). [lean/Ehr/Specs/V1.lean:approveRefillReviews]

Hospital policy (not law): local dose, glucose and coagulation review protocol; receipt windows are product design.
Before giving a medication: the allergy list (shown, or reviewed by a nurse
this 12-hour shift), the dose details (always shown), recent glucose before
insulin, and coagulation labs before an anticoagulant.

Our hospital review policy; list display, glucose/coagulation display and the 12 h receipt window are not statutory mandates (docs/regulations.md R2, H3, H5). [lean/Ehr/Specs/V1.lean:administerMedReviews]

Hospital policy (not law): local allergy and immunization-history review protocol; receipt windows are product design.
Before recording a vaccine: the allergy list and the immunization history
(shown, or reviewed by a vaccinator in the last 12 hours).

Our safety review and receipt policy, beyond the federal vaccine record and VIS duties (docs/regulations.md E11). [lean/Ehr/Specs/V1.lean:recordImmunizationReviews]

Hospital policy (not law): local discharge medication review; receipt window is product design.
Before discharge: the current medications (always shown, for discharge
reconciliation) and the allergy list (shown, or reviewed in the last 4 hours).

Our display/receipt implementation of reconciliation and discharge duties; see CA H&S 1262.5 and TJC medication reconciliation (docs/regulations.md E10, E16). [lean/Ehr/Specs/V1.lean:dischargeReviews]

Every review requirement in spec v1. [lean/Ehr/Specs/V1.lean:reviews]

Interruptive: an ordered drug matches one of the patient's allergies. [lean/Ehr/Specs/V1.lean:drugAllergyOrderAlert]

Interruptive: an ordered drug has a severe interaction with a current medication. [lean/Ehr/Specs/V1.lean:interactionOrderAlert]

Interruptive: the refilled drug has a severe interaction with a current medication. [lean/Ehr/Specs/V1.lean:interactionRefillAlert]

Interruptive: an ordered dose is outside the usual range for today's weight. [lean/Ehr/Specs/V1.lean:doseRangeOrderAlert]

Interruptive: the weight was entered in pounds. It must be re-entered in kg. [lean/Ehr/Specs/V1.lean:weightInLbAlert]

Passive: the patient has care gaps (screenings due). Shown inline throughout the adult workflow. [lean/Ehr/Specs/V1.lean:careGapsInlineAlert]

Passive: an ordered drug duplicates a current therapy (low severity).
Shown inline throughout the adult workflow. [lean/Ehr/Specs/V1.lean:duplicateTherapyInlineAlert]

Passive: vaccines are due. Shown inline throughout the pediatric workflow. [lean/Ehr/Specs/V1.lean:immunizationsDueInlineAlert]

Removable: a "health maintenance reminder" popup when closing a visit with
care gaps. The same gaps are already shown inline. [lean/Ehr/Specs/V1.lean:healthMaintenancePopupAlert]

Removable: a "high-alert medication ordered, review policy" notice when
signing orders. The nurse's double check is the real safeguard. [lean/Ehr/Specs/V1.lean:highAlertOrderNoticeAlert]

Removable: "patient already has an active prescription for this drug" when
refilling. A refill always matches an active prescription. [lean/Ehr/Specs/V1.lean:activeRxPopupAlert]

Removable: a care-gap reminder popup during a refill. [lean/Ehr/Specs/V1.lean:refillCareGapPopupAlert]

New artifact paths cannot bypass ordinary signing. [lean/Ehr/Specs/V1.lean:inheritedEntries]

Inherited reviews keep their exact modes and waivers. [lean/Ehr/Specs/V1.lean:inheritedReviews]

All issuance variants retain each signing alert;
only ids and commit bindings differ. Triggers and tiers remain unchanged. [lean/Ehr/Specs/V1.lean:inheritedAlerts]

Specialist issuance also inherits every new
prescription-content gate and every controlled-prescription display rule. [lean/Ehr/Specs/V1.lean:inheritedRuleEntries]

No specialist signing action bypasses controlled details. [lean/Ehr/Specs/V1.lean:inheritedRuleReviews]

Catalog commits kept in core because tested workflows need them: W7 denials. [lean/Ehr/Specs/V1.lean:coreCatalogCommits]

Hand-written rules and workflow exceptions, plus the full catalog on request. [lean/Ehr/Specs/V1.lean:specForProfile]

Hospital policy (not law): local antibiotic indication policy modeled on CDC stewardship guidance; 42 CFR 482.42(b) and CA H&S 1288.85 require stewardship programs, not these per-order fields.
Confirmed coded indication for each antibiotic order. Stewardship programs
are required by 42 CFR 482.42(b) and CA H&S 1288.85. A per-order indication
is this hospital's hypothetical policy, modeled on CDC Core Elements (2019),
not a statutory per-order mandate. Surgeons prescribe prophylaxis too.
See docs/regulations.md §5.1. [lean/Ehr/Specs/V3.lean:abxIndication]

Hospital policy (not law): local antibiotic indication policy modeled on CDC stewardship guidance; 42 CFR 482.42(b) and CA H&S 1288.85 require stewardship programs, not these per-order fields.
The active antibiotic's indication beside the MAR dose. Showing it before
administration, with no receipt, is our own additional policy (no source found
requiring it); docs/regulations.md §3.2 R7 and §5.1. [lean/Ehr/Specs/V3.lean:abxIndicationInfo]

Hospital policy (not law): local antibiotic indication policy modeled on CDC stewardship guidance; 42 CFR 482.42(b) and CA H&S 1288.85 require stewardship programs, not these per-order fields.
R2-1/R2-4: every antibiotic line needs its own confirmed indication on
ordinary signing, including pre-op encounters. [lean/Ehr/Specs/V3.lean:antibioticEntries]

Release v3 preserves all v2 rules and adds stewardship obligations. [lean/Ehr/Specs/V3.lean:specV3]

